WN.lb(37,{"mt_Jvg_r4yWaY":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"On a smooth ramp a skater starts still with {=g*10} J in her gravitational store. How many joules are in her kinetic store at the very bottom?","answer":"g*10","hint":"No friction: all of it swaps over.","explain":"All {=g*10} J moves from the gravitational store to the kinetic store.","params":{"g":[10,90]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You charge a phone. Which description is right?","choices":["Energy is transferred electrically into the battery's chemical store","Electricity is stored as electricity in the phone","Energy is made inside the charger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Batteries store energy in chemicals.","explain":"The current carries energy (electrically) and it is stored in the battery's chemical store."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A sledge starts with 500 J. At the bottom of a rough hill it has {=k*10} J in its kinetic store and nothing in its gravitational store. How many joules went to thermal stores?","answer":"500-k*10","hint":"The total is still 500 J.","explain":"500 − {=k*10} = {=500-k*10} J went to thermal stores by heating.","params":{"k":[10,45]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two skaters, 40 kg and 80 kg, roll from the same height on a smooth ramp. At the bottom…","choices":["they have the same speed","the heavier one is twice as fast","the lighter one is twice as fast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twice the mass gives twice the energy, but also twice as much to speed up.","explain":"mgh = ½mv², so the mass cancels: v = √(2gh). Same height, same speed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A hot cup of tea cools down on the table. Which store empties, and by which pathway?","choices":["Its thermal store, by heating the air and table (and some radiation)","Its chemical store, electrically","Its kinetic store, mechanically"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the tea losing? How does a hot thing warm what is around it?","explain":"The tea's thermal store empties by heating the cooler air and table, and a little by infrared radiation."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A {m} kg trolley moves at {v} m/s. How many joules are in its kinetic store (½mv²)?","answer":"m*v*v/2","hint":"Square the speed first, then multiply by the mass and halve.","explain":"½ × {m} × {v}² = {=m*v*v/2} J.","params":{"m":[2,6],"v":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"How does energy from the Sun reach Earth through empty space?","choices":["By radiation (light and infrared)","By heating the air on the way","Electrically"],"answer":0,"hint":"Space has almost no air to heat.","explain":"Only radiation can cross empty space. The light and infrared then warm the ground's thermal store."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A stretched catapult is about to fire. Which store holds most of its energy?","choices":["Elastic potential","Kinetic","Chemical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nothing is moving yet. What has been stretched?","explain":"Stretched or squashed things store energy in an elastic potential store. When it fires, that energy moves to the kinetic store of the stone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A skater on a smooth ramp has {=t*10} J in total. Right now her kinetic store is {=k*10} J. How many joules are in her gravitational store?","answer":"t*10-k*10","hint":"No friction, so the total stays the same. Total − kinetic = ?","explain":"{=t*10} − {=k*10} = {=t*10-k*10} J in the gravitational store.","params":{"t":[20,60],"k":[5,50]},"where":"k<t"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bike skids to a stop. Its kinetic store is now empty. Where did that energy go?","choices":["Into thermal stores of the tyre, road and air, by heating","It was destroyed by the brakes","Back into the rider's chemical store"],"answer":0,"hint":"Feel a tyre just after a long skid.","explain":"Friction transfers the energy by heating. The tyre, road and air warm up a little. The total is the same; it is just spread out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A {m} kg box is lifted {h} m onto a shelf. Using g = 10 N/kg, how much energy goes into its gravitational store (mgh)?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"Multiply mass × 10 × height.","explain":"{m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[1,9],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Put the energy journey in a torch in order.","items":["Chemical store of the battery","Transferred electrically by the current","Bulb heats up and radiates light","Thermal store of the surroundings"],"hint":"Start where the energy is stored before you switch on.","explain":"Chemical store → electrically → the bulb heats and gives out light → the light is absorbed, warming the surroundings."}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of energy like money in different bank accounts. The accounts are called stores. Moving things have a kinetic store, and high things have a gravitational store. Hot things have a thermal store, and food and fuel have a chemical store.","Energy moves from one store to another. A force can push it (mechanically), and a current can carry it (electrically). A hot thing can warm a cold one (heating), and light or sound can carry it away (radiation).","The total never changes. When a skater slows down on a rough ramp, the energy is not gone. It has gone into warm wheels, a warm ramp and warm air."],"adult":["The store model is bookkeeping: pick a start and an end, list which stores went up and which went down, and the changes must add to zero. Energy is conserved; it just becomes less useful as it spreads into thermal stores.","The stores to know are kinetic (½mv²), gravitational potential (mgh), elastic, thermal, chemical, nuclear, magnetic and electrostatic. The pathways are mechanical work, electrical work, heating and radiation.","A common slip is saying energy is 'used up' or 'lost'. Better: it is transferred to the surroundings, usually by heating, where we can't easily use it again."]},"familyTip":"Drop a ball from knee height and count how many bounces it makes. Each bounce is lower. Ask: where did the energy go? Feel the ball after many fast bounces on a hard floor.","keyIdea":"Energy is never made or destroyed. It sits in stores (kinetic, thermal, chemical and more) and moves between them mechanically, electrically, by heating or by radiation.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Where is the energy mostly stored? Drag each card into a store, or tap a card and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Kinetic","Gravitational","Chemical","Elastic"],"items":[["A rolling bowling ball",0],["A cyclist at top speed",0],["A book on a high shelf",1],["Water behind a dam",1],["A slice of toast",2],["A charged battery",2],["A stretched rubber band",3],["A squashed spring",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagenergy","caption":"Flagship lab: the skate ramp. Let the skater go and watch energy move between stores, but the total never change.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2bnXrfS4Iq":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Two identical bulbs in one loop each have {h} V across them. What is the battery's voltage?","answer":"h*2","hint":"Around a loop the voltages add up to the battery's.","explain":"{h} + {h} = {=h*2} V.","params":{"h":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does a current of 2 A mean?","choices":["2 coulombs of charge pass each second","2 joules of energy per charge","The battery has 2 volts"],"answer":0,"hint":"Current is a rate of flow.","explain":"1 amp = 1 coulomb per second, so 2 A means 2 coulombs of charge flow past each second."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sam puts a voltmeter into the loop, in series with a bulb. What happens?","choices":["The bulb goes out, because a voltmeter hardly lets current through","The bulb gets brighter","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"A voltmeter has a very high resistance.","explain":"A voltmeter has a huge resistance, so in the loop it almost stops the current. The bulb goes out."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"An ammeter in a loop reads {=a/10} A. A second ammeter, after two bulbs, is added. What does it read, in A?","answer":"a/10","hint":"In one loop, is current shared out or the same everywhere?","explain":"The same: {=a/10} A. Current is the same at every point in a single loop.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You add a second identical bulb to a one-bulb loop with the same battery. What happens?","choices":["Both bulbs are dimmer: each gets half the voltage and less current flows","Both stay just as bright","The first bulb gets brighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two bulbs share the battery's push.","explain":"The voltage is shared, and with more resistance the current falls, so both bulbs are dimmer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best description of voltage?","choices":["The energy given to or taken from each unit of charge","How much charge flows each second","How fast the charges move"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its unit, the volt, is a joule per coulomb.","explain":"Voltage (potential difference) is energy per unit charge: 1 V = 1 J per coulomb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A torch needs {v} V. Each cell gives 1.5 V. How many cells must be put in a row?","answer":"v/1.5","hint":"Divide the voltage needed by 1.5.","explain":"{v} ÷ 1.5 = {=v/1.5} cells.","params":{"v":[3,15]},"where":"v%3==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An ammeter before a bulb reads 0.4 A. What does an ammeter just after the bulb read?","choices":["0.4 A","Less than 0.4 A","0 A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is charge used up in a bulb?","explain":"0.4 A. In a single loop the current is the same everywhere. The bulb takes energy from the charges, not the charges themselves."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A battery is made of {n} cells in a row, each 1.5 V. What is the battery's voltage?","answer":"n*1.5","hint":"Cells in a row add their voltages.","explain":"{n} × 1.5 = {=n*1.5} V.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How should a voltmeter be connected to measure the voltage of a bulb?","choices":["Across the bulb (in parallel)","In the loop next to the bulb (in series)","It doesn't matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"It compares the energy before and after the part.","explain":"Across the bulb, in parallel. It measures the energy each charge gives up between the two sides of the bulb."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A {v} V battery lights {b} identical bulbs in one loop. What is the voltage across each bulb?","answer":"v/b","hint":"The bulbs share the battery's voltage equally.","explain":"{v} ÷ {b} = {=v/b} V across each bulb.","params":{"v":[3,12],"b":[2,3]},"where":"v%b==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit is a loop. Tiny charges flow all the way round it. The battery gives each charge a push and some energy.","Current is how much charge goes past a point each second. We measure it in amps (A) with an ammeter. It sits in the loop.","Voltage is how much energy each charge drops off at a part, like a bulb. We measure it in volts (V) with a voltmeter. It sits across the part, on its own side loop.","A bulb does not use up current. The same current comes out as goes in. The bulb takes energy, not charge."],"adult":["Current is the rate of flow of charge: I = Q ÷ t, so 1 A means 1 coulomb per second. In a single loop it is the same at every point, which is why an ammeter can go anywhere in series.","Potential difference is energy transferred per unit charge: V = E ÷ Q, so 1 V means 1 joule per coulomb. Around a series loop the voltages across the parts add up to the battery's voltage.","Ammeters have almost no resistance and go in series; voltmeters have a very high resistance and go in parallel. In metal wires the charges that move are electrons, drifting the opposite way to the conventional current arrow."]},"familyTip":"Look on a phone charger or a battery: it gives the voltage (for example 5 V or 1.5 V). Look at a kettle plug label for the current (for example 13 A). Talk about which one is a push and which one is a flow.","keyIdea":"Current is how fast charge flows round a circuit, measured in amps with an ammeter in the loop. Voltage (potential difference) is the energy each charge gives up or gains, measured in volts with a voltmeter across a part.","visual":{"type":"circuit","caption":"Current only flows round a complete loop of conductors. Put each object in the gap: does the bulb light?","opts":{"items":[["Copper wire",1],["Steel paper clip",1],["Aluminium foil",1],["Pencil lead (graphite)",1],["Plastic straw",0],["Wooden lolly stick",0],["Glass marble",0],["Rubber band",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcirc","caption":"Flagship lab: the circuit bench. Move the meters, add cells and bulbs, and watch charge and energy flow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0DyF7bl4zh":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A {m} kg rock sits on a ledge {h} m above the ground (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy is in its gravitational store, in J?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"E = m × g × h, with g = 10 N/kg on Earth.","explain":"E = {m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[2,8],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You lift a box to twice the height. What happens to the energy in its gravitational store?","choices":["It doubles","It stays the same","It goes up by 4 times"],"answer":0,"hint":"E = m × g × h. What happens to the answer if h doubles?","explain":"Energy is proportional to height, so twice the height gives twice the energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A {g} g apple is lifted 2 m (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy is stored, in J? Change grams to kilograms first.","answer":"g/1000*10*2","hint":"Divide the grams by 1000 to get kilograms, then use E = m × g × h.","explain":"{g} g = {=g/1000} kg, so E = {=g/1000} × 10 × 2 = {=g/1000*10*2} J.","params":{"g":[100,500]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A ball is dropped. Ignoring air resistance, where does the energy in its gravitational store go?","choices":["Into its kinetic store","It is used up","It stays in the gravitational store"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ball gets lower and faster. Which store gets bigger as it speeds up?","explain":"Energy is not used up. As the ball falls, the gravitational store empties into the kinetic store, so it speeds up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A crate is lifted from a 2 m shelf to a 5 m shelf. Which height do you use in E = mgh?","choices":["3 m","5 m","7 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"The store changes by the height gained, not the final height.","explain":"Height gained = 5 m − 2 m = 3 m. Using 5 m or adding to 7 m are the common slips."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A 4 kg crate and a 1 kg crate sit on the same shelf. How does the energy in their gravitational stores compare?","choices":["The 4 kg crate has 4 times as much","They have the same","The 1 kg crate has more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Energy is proportional to mass.","explain":"Same g and same height, but 4 times the mass, so 4 times the energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"On the Moon, g = 1.6 N/kg. A 5 kg box is lifted {h} m. How much energy is stored, in J?","answer":"5*1.6*h","hint":"E = m × g × h, with g = 1.6 instead of 10.","explain":"E = 5 × 1.6 × {h} = {=5*1.6*h} J. On Earth the same lift would store {=5*10*h} J.","params":{"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A 2 kg crate stores 60 J in its gravitational store on Earth (g = 10 N/kg). How high is it, in metres?","answer":3,"hint":"Rearrange E = m × g × h: h = E ÷ (m × g).","explain":"h = 60 ÷ (2 × 10) = 60 ÷ 20 = 3 m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Why does a heavy bag lifted up the stairs store more energy than a light bag lifted the same height?","choices":["It has more mass, and E is proportional to m","It took longer to carry","The stairs are steeper for it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which letter in E = m × g × h is different for the two bags?","explain":"g and h are the same for both, so the bag with more mass stores more energy. Time and steepness do not appear in the equation."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A {m} kg box is lifted {h} m on Earth (g = 10 N/kg). How much energy goes into its gravitational store, in joules?","answer":"m*10*h","hint":"Use E = m × g × h. Multiply the mass by 10, then by the height.","explain":"E = {m} × 10 × {h} = {=m*10*h} J.","params":{"m":[1,6],"h":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A {m} kg bag is moved from a 1 m shelf up to a {h} m shelf (g = 10 N/kg). How much does its gravitational store increase, in J?","answer":"m*10*(h-1)","hint":"Find the height gained first: the new height minus 1 m. Then use E = m × g × h.","explain":"Height gained = {h} − 1 = {=h-1} m, so E = {m} × 10 × {=h-1} = {=m*10*(h-1)} J.","params":{"m":[1,5],"h":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The same 2 kg crate is lifted 3 m on Earth and on the Moon. Why does it store less energy on the Moon?","choices":["g is smaller on the Moon","Its mass is smaller on the Moon","Energy cannot be stored on the Moon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Mass is the amount of matter. Does that change when you fly to the Moon?","explain":"The mass stays 2 kg. The gravitational field strength drops from 10 to 1.6 N/kg, so m × g × h is smaller."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Two people take the same box to the same shelf. One uses a long ramp, one lifts it straight up. Ignoring friction, which box stores more energy?","choices":["They store the same","The one pushed up the ramp","The one lifted straight up"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, every route to the 3 m shelf gave the same bar.","explain":"Only the mass, g and the height gained matter. Both boxes gained the same height, so they store the same energy."}],"explain":{"kid":["When you lift a bag onto a shelf, you do work against gravity. That energy is not lost. It goes into the gravitational store of the bag and the Earth.","The amount depends on three things. A heavier bag stores more. A higher shelf stores more. And stronger gravity stores more. So E = m × g × h. On Earth, g is about 10 N/kg.","Only the change in height counts. Lift a box from a 1 m shelf to a 3 m shelf and the height gained is 2 m. The route does not matter. A ramp and a straight lift to the same shelf store the same energy.","On the Moon, g is only 1.6 N/kg. The same lift stores much less energy. When something falls, its gravitational store empties into its kinetic store, so it speeds up. Ignoring air, all of it ends up as kinetic energy at the ground."],"adult":["This is Key Stage 3 physics in England. The energy topic asks pupils to treat energy as a quantity that can be calculated and to describe changes in a system, including changes in position in a field. The forces topic gives weight = mass × gravitational field strength, with g = 10 N/kg on Earth and different values on other planets. GCSE adds the equation Ep = m g h, often with g = 9.8 N/kg. In the US, NGSS MS-PS3-2 asks students to model how potential energy changes when objects interacting at a distance change their arrangement.","Two ideas carry most of the reasoning. First, the energy is proportional to each of m, g and h, so doubling one doubles the store. Second, only the change in vertical height matters. Ignoring friction, the path taken does not change the energy stored. The lab uses g = 10 N/kg on Earth, 3.7 N/kg on Mars and 1.6 N/kg on the Moon.","Common mistakes: using the final height instead of the height gained, or adding the two heights. Leaving g out and multiplying only mass by height. Using grams or centimetres without converting to kilograms and metres. Thinking a longer ramp stores more energy. And saying the energy is used up when an object falls, instead of transferred to the kinetic store. Be careful with the Moon too: the mass is the same there, but g is smaller, so the energy is smaller.","In the lab students lift a crate up a 5 m scale while a bar shows the gravitational store, swap 1, 2 and 4 kg crates at the same height, and send a crate to the same shelf by a straight lift, stairs and a ramp. They repeat a lift on Earth, Mars and the Moon, then let the crate fall and watch the gravitational store drain into the kinetic store."]},"familyTip":"Carry a bag of shopping up the stairs together and ask where the energy went. Then ask how you could get it back (drop or roll something down). Try the numbers: a 2 kg bag lifted 3 m stores 2 × 10 × 3 = 60 J.","keyIdea":"Lifting an object stores energy in its gravitational store. The energy stored is E = m × g × h: mass (kg) times gravitational field strength (N/kg) times the height gained (m). Double the mass or the height and the energy doubles. Weaker gravity, as on the Moon, stores less. When the object falls, that energy moves to the kinetic store.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"More energy in the gravitational store, or less? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["⬆️ More energy stored","⬇️ Less energy stored"],"items":[["Lift the same crate twice as high",0],["Do the same lift on the Moon",1],["Use a crate with half the mass",1],["Lift a heavier crate to the same shelf",0],["Lower the crate to a shelf nearer the floor",1],["Do the same lift where g is stronger",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglift","caption":"Flagship lab: lift a crate and watch E = mgh, change the mass, try three routes, lift on Earth, Mars and the Moon, then let it fall.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_s9Whgzsav0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which has the most kinetic energy?","choices":["A 2 kg ball at 1 m/s","A 1 kg ball at 3 m/s","A 4 kg ball at 1 m/s","A 10 kg ball standing still"],"answer":1,"hint":"Mass counts once. Speed counts twice. Something still has none.","explain":"The 1 kg ball at 3 m/s has ½ × 1 × 9 = 4.5 J. The 4 kg ball at 1 m/s has 2 J, the 2 kg ball 1 J, and the still ball 0 J."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A car speeds up from 30 mph to 60 mph. Compared with before, its kinetic energy is…","choices":["the same","2 times as big","4 times as big","60 times as big"],"answer":2,"hint":"Twice the speed. Square the 2.","explain":"Twice the speed means 2 × 2 = 4 times the kinetic energy. That is why crashes at high speed do so much more damage."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A parked lorry has a mass of 40 000 kg. What is its kinetic energy?","choices":["Zero","40 000 J","Very large, because it is heavy","Half its mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kinetic energy is energy of movement.","explain":"It is not moving, so it has no kinetic energy, however heavy it is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says, 'Doubling speed doubles kinetic energy, just like mass.' What is wrong?","choices":["Nothing, Sam is right","Speed is squared, so doubling speed gives four times the energy","Speed does not change kinetic energy","Doubling mass gives four times the energy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at 1 m/s and 2 m/s for the same cart: 1 J and 4 J.","explain":"Mass counts once, so it doubles the energy. Speed is squared, so doubling it gives four times the energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A car going at 10 m/s and a lorry with 10 times its mass going at 3 m/s. Which has more kinetic energy?","choices":["The car","The lorry","They are the same","You cannot tell from mass and speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the multipliers: mass ×10 for the lorry, speed squared for the car.","explain":"Compare mass × speed². Car: 1 × 10 × 10 = 100. Lorry: 10 × 3 × 3 = 90. The car has slightly more, even though the lorry is 10 times heavier."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why are 20 mph zones used near schools instead of 30 mph?","choices":["Cars are lighter at 20 mph","At 20 mph a car has under half the kinetic energy it has at 30 mph, so crashes do far less harm","Kinetic energy does not depend on speed","Slower cars have more energy"],"answer":1,"hint":"Compare 2 × 2 with 3 × 3.","explain":"Energy goes with speed squared: 20² is 400 and 30² is 900, so 20 mph has less than half the crash energy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A ball has {e} J of kinetic energy. Its speed is doubled. How many joules does it have now?","answer":"4*e","hint":"Double the speed means four times the energy.","explain":"2 × 2 = 4, so {e} J becomes {=4*e} J.","params":{"e":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A trolley's mass is multiplied by {k}. Its speed stays the same. How many times bigger is its kinetic energy?","answer":"k","hint":"Mass and kinetic energy go up in step.","explain":"Mass counts once, so {k} times the mass gives {k} times the energy.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A runner's speed is multiplied by {k}. How many times bigger is her kinetic energy?","answer":"k*k","hint":"Multiply the speed change by itself.","explain":"{k} × {k} = {=k*k}, so her kinetic energy is {=k*k} times bigger.","params":{"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A shopping trolley moves at 1 m/s. You fill it so its mass doubles, and push it at the same speed. Its kinetic energy…","choices":["stays the same","doubles","goes up four times","halves"],"answer":1,"hint":"Mass and kinetic energy go up in step.","explain":"Twice the mass at the same speed gives twice the kinetic energy. Only speed is squared."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A cyclist goes {k} times faster than before. Her mass stays the same. How many times bigger is her kinetic energy?","answer":"k*k","hint":"Speed is squared: multiply the change by itself.","explain":"{k} times the speed gives {k} × {k} = {=k*k} times the kinetic energy.","params":{"k":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A table shows a cart's kinetic energy at 1, 2 and 3 m/s: 1 J, 4 J and 9 J. What will the graph of energy against speed look like?","choices":["A straight line through the points","A curve that gets steeper as speed goes up","A flat line","A curve that levels off"],"answer":1,"hint":"Look at how much the energy goes up for each extra 1 m/s.","explain":"The jumps are 3 J, then 5 J. Each extra 1 m/s adds more energy than the last, so the curve gets steeper."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A cart has {e} J of kinetic energy. You load it to {k} times its mass and keep the same speed. How many joules does it have now?","answer":"e*k","hint":"Mass multiplies the energy once.","explain":"{k} times the mass gives {k} times the energy: {e} × {k} = {=e*k} J.","params":{"e":[2,9],"k":[2,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Kinetic energy is the energy something has because it is moving. A still object has none. The faster it goes, or the heavier it is, the more it has. You can see this in a crash: more energy means a bigger dent.","Mass and energy go up in step. Put twice as much in a trolley, push it at the same speed, and it has twice the energy. Four times the mass gives four times the energy.","Speed counts more. Twice the speed gives four times the energy, because 2 × 2 = 4. Three times the speed gives nine times the energy. So on a graph of energy against speed, the line bends upward and gets steeper.","This is why speed limits matter. A car at 60 mph has four times the energy of the same car at 30 mph. In a crash, all that energy goes into bending metal and hurting people."],"adult":["This lesson meets NGSS MS-PS3-1: learners construct and interpret data to describe how kinetic energy depends on mass and on speed. The formula is kinetic energy = ½ × mass × speed², but at this stage the aim is the shape of the relationship (proportional to mass, proportional to speed squared), read from tables, graphs and a cart model rather than lengthy calculation.","The most common misconception is that doubling the speed doubles the energy. Learners treat speed like mass. Ask them to compare 1 m/s and 2 m/s for the same cart in the data, where the energy goes from 1 J to 4 J. A second slip is to think a heavy object always has more energy, even when it is moving slowly; the car against lorry and football against cricket ball comparisons show speed can win.","Be careful with graphs: some learners draw a straight line through the first two points and extrapolate. The table step asks them to predict 3 m/s and 4 m/s before the curve appears. Also make sure they know that a parked object has zero kinetic energy, however heavy it is, and that energy is measured in joules (J).","For road safety, connect the maths to real decisions: going from 20 to 30 mph raises crash energy about 2.25 times, which is why 20 mph zones near schools reduce serious injuries. Ask your learner to estimate how much more energy a car carries at 40 mph than at 20 mph."]},"familyTip":"Roll a toy car down a ramp from low and then higher up, so it goes faster, into a pile of paper cups. Then tape a coin or two on top and roll it from the low spot again. Which made the bigger mess: more mass, or more speed?","keyIdea":"Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because it is moving. It increases in proportion to mass: twice the mass at the same speed means twice the kinetic energy. It increases with the square of speed: twice the speed means four times the kinetic energy, and three times the speed means nine times. So a graph of kinetic energy against speed curves upward, and small increases in speed cause much bigger increases in crash energy.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What happens to the kinetic energy? Drag each change to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Doubles","Four times as big","No change"],"items":[["Double the mass, same speed",0],["Double the speed, same mass",1],["Paint it a new colour",2],["Four times the mass, same speed",1],["Halve the mass, double the speed",0],["Turn round, same speed",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagkemv","caption":"Flagship lab: load a cart with mass, change its speed, fill a data table, compare moving objects and crash-test a car.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_d-EKO-pKkP":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A tray holds {a} coins, {b} wooden sticks and {d} rubber bands. How many objects are conductors?","answer":"a","hint":"Only one of these materials is a metal.","explain":"Coins are metal, so they conduct. Wood and rubber are insulators. That makes {a} conductors.","params":{"a":[2,7],"b":[1,5],"d":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A class tests {n} objects. {k} keep the bulb off. How many are conductors?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Objects that keep the bulb off are insulators. The rest are conductors.","explain":"{n} take away {k} insulators leaves {=n-k} conductors.","params":{"n":[9,14],"k":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these is an insulator?","choices":["a plastic ruler","an iron nail","aluminium foil"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that is not a metal.","explain":"Plastic blocks electricity, so a plastic ruler is an insulator. The nail and the foil are metals and conduct."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Zoe says a shiny plastic bead will light the bulb because it is shiny. Is she right?","choices":["No, it is plastic, so it is an insulator","Yes, shiny things conduct","Yes, but only a little"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the bead made of?","explain":"Shiny is not the clue. The bead is plastic, which is an insulator, so the bulb stays off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A clip touches only the plastic coat of a wire. What happens?","choices":["The bulb stays off","The bulb lights","The battery gets stronger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of the wire is the conductor: the coat or the core?","explain":"Only the copper core conducts. The plastic coat is an insulator, so no electricity flows and the bulb stays off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Most good conductors of electricity are…","choices":["metals","heavy things","hard things"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the paper clip, the coin and the foil. What do they have in common?","explain":"Metals are good conductors. Heavy or hard things, such as a stone or a wooden block, can still be insulators."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A wooden stick keeps the bulb off. Sam says the battery is flat. How can he check?","choices":["Put a metal key in the gap","Shake the bulb","Use a longer stick"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test with something you already know is a conductor.","explain":"If a metal key lights the bulb, the battery works. That shows the wood is an insulator."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why are cables made of copper inside and plastic outside?","choices":["Copper carries the electricity and plastic keeps it off our hands","Plastic carries the electricity and copper makes it strong","Both help electricity flow faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is the conductor and which is the insulator?","explain":"Copper is a metal conductor, so it carries the current. Plastic is an insulator, so it keeps people safe."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A steel spoon goes in the gap and the bulb lights. What is steel?","choices":["A conductor","An insulator","A battery"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did electricity get through the spoon to the bulb?","explain":"The bulb lit, so electricity flowed through the steel. Steel is a metal and a conductor."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ravi tests {n} objects. {k} of them light the bulb. How many objects are insulators?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Every object that did not light the bulb is an insulator. Take the conductors away from the total.","explain":"{n} objects take away {k} conductors leaves {=n-k} insulators.","params":{"n":[8,12],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A box has {m} metal objects and {p} plastic objects. How many will light the bulb?","answer":"m","hint":"Which of the two materials is a conductor?","explain":"Metals conduct and plastic does not, so the {m} metal objects light the bulb.","params":{"m":[2,9],"p":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is the handle of an electrician's screwdriver made of rubber or plastic?","choices":["They are insulators, so electricity cannot reach the hand","They are conductors, so electricity flows better","They are shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of material blocks electricity?","explain":"Rubber and plastic are insulators. They stop electricity flowing into the person holding the tool."}],"explain":{"kid":["A bulb lights only when electricity can flow all the way round a loop, from the battery, through the bulb and back. Leave a gap and the flow stops. Put an object in the gap and you can test it.","If the bulb lights, the object is a conductor. It lets electricity through. If the bulb stays off, the object is an insulator. It blocks the flow.","Metals are good conductors. A steel paper clip, a coin, foil and an iron nail all light the bulb. Plastic, rubber and wood do not. A shiny plastic bead stays off, so shiny is not the clue. Ask: is it metal?","A wire has a copper core inside a plastic coat. The copper carries the electricity. The plastic is an insulator, so it keeps the electricity off your hands. Only test things with a small battery, never with a plug socket."],"adult":["This is a Year 4 topic in the national curriculum for England. Pupils should recognise some common conductors and insulators. They should also link metals with being good conductors. It builds on making a simple series circuit, where a bulb lights only in a complete loop. The same unit asks pupils to set up simple fair tests.","Metals conduct because some of their electrons are free to move. In plastic, rubber, dry wood and glass the electrons are held in place, so almost no current flows. The test circuit is a fair test: only the object in the gap changes. If a metal key lights the bulb, the cell and bulb work. So an object that leaves it dark is an insulator.","Common mistakes: thinking shiny means conductor, so a shiny plastic bead should work. Thinking hard or heavy things conduct. Thinking a wire conducts wherever you touch it, even on its plastic coat. Thinking the coat helps the current flow. Blaming a flat battery when the gap holds an insulator. Pencil lead (graphite) is a non-metal that conducts a little, so the bulb may glow dimly. Save it as an extra once the metal rule is secure.","In the lab learners close a gap with a steel key and test six everyday objects. They test a shiny plastic bead against a dull iron nail. They clip onto a wire's plastic coat, then onto its stripped copper end. Last, they pick conductors and insulators for a cable, a plug and a screwdriver."]},"familyTip":"With a 1.5 V battery, a torch bulb and two wires with clips, test things from a drawer: a spoon, a key, a pencil, a lolly stick, foil, an eraser. Predict first, then test. Never test anything with a plug socket, which can kill.","keyIdea":"An electrical conductor is a material that lets electricity flow through it, so it completes a circuit and the bulb lights. An electrical insulator blocks the flow, so the bulb stays off. Metals such as copper, steel, iron and aluminium are good conductors; plastic, rubber, wood and glass are insulators. Shiny, hard or heavy is not the clue: test the material. Wires have a metal core to carry the electricity and a plastic coat to keep it off your hands.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Conductor or insulator? Drag each object, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Conductor: lets electricity through","Insulator: blocks electricity"],"items":[["steel paper clip",0],["plastic spoon",1],["aluminium foil",0],["rubber band",1],["iron nail",0],["wooden lolly stick",1],["copper wire",0],["glass marble",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcond","caption":"Flagship lab: test objects in the gap of a circuit, sort conductors from insulators, see why shiny is not the clue and why wires have a coat.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nsZhXdxV14":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is stronger, so it can hurt you badly?","choices":["A wall socket","A torch battery","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the two bars in the lab. Which one was huge?","explain":"A wall socket gives over 100 volts. A torch battery gives about 1.5 volts, so it is safe to hold."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you unplug a lamp the safe way?","choices":["Switch off, then pull the plug","Pull hard on the cable","Pull it out while it is on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you hold, and what do you do first?","explain":"Switch off at the wall, then hold the plug and pull. Pulling the cable can break the wires inside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Your toast is stuck in the toaster. What do you do?","choices":["Ask a grown-up to unplug it","Get it out with a fork","Shake it while it is on"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is metal a safe thing to put inside a toaster?","explain":"A metal fork can carry electricity to your hand. A grown-up must switch off and unplug the toaster first."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your kite is stuck in a power line. What do you do?","choices":["Let go and tell a grown-up","Climb up to get it","Pull the string hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the string carry electricity down to you?","explain":"Let go and tell a grown-up. The string can carry electricity, and you must never climb a pole or pylon."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A remote needs 2 batteries. A toy car needs {k} batteries. How many batteries for both?","answer":"2+k","hint":"Start at 2 and count on {k} more.","explain":"2 batteries for the remote and {k} for the car make {=2+k} batteries.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You see a cable with the wires showing. What do you do?","choices":["Do not touch it and tell a grown-up","Wrap it in paper","Plug it in to see if it works"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a broken cable safe to touch?","explain":"Bare wires can give you an electric shock. Do not touch it, and tell a grown-up so they can deal with it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"There are {t} sockets in a room. {u} have a plug in. How many are empty?","answer":"t-u","hint":"Start at {t} and take away the {u} with plugs.","explain":"{t} take away {u} leaves {=t-u} empty sockets.","params":{"t":[4,8],"u":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why should a hairdryer be kept away from the bath and the sink?","choices":["Water can carry electricity to you","It makes the water too hot","It will dry up the water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about water and electricity together.","explain":"Water and electricity do not mix. Water can carry the electricity to you and give you a shock."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Lots of plugs are pushed into one socket. Why is that a problem?","choices":["It can get hot and start a fire","The lights will get brighter","Nothing, it is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened to the crowded socket in the lab?","explain":"Too many plugs in one socket can overheat and start a fire. Ask a grown-up to unplug some."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one gets its electricity from a socket in the wall?","choices":["A toaster","A torch","A remote"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one with a cable and a plug.","explain":"A toaster has a cable and a plug, so it uses mains electricity. A torch and a remote use batteries."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A torch needs 2 batteries. You have {n} torches. How many batteries do you need?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count in 2s, once for each torch.","explain":"Each torch needs 2 batteries, so {n} torches need {=2*n} batteries.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In the kitchen, {a} things plug in and {b} things use batteries. How many things is that in all?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more.","explain":"{a} plug-in things and {b} battery things make {=a+b} things.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your hands are wet from washing. You want to turn on the light. What do you do?","choices":["Dry my hands first","Be very quick","Use one wet finger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does being quick stop the water carrying electricity?","explain":"Water can carry electricity to you, so dry your hands first. Being quick does not help."}],"explain":{"kid":["A lamp has a cable and a plug. It gets electricity from the wall. A torch has batteries inside.","Electricity from the wall is very strong. It can hurt you badly. A small battery is weak and safe to hold.","Water and electricity do not mix. Dry your hands before you touch a plug or a switch.","Never poke a fork, a finger or a toy into a socket or a toaster. Tell a grown-up if a cable is broken. Fly your kite in an open park, far from power lines."],"adult":["In England, the Year 4 science unit on electricity asks children to name everyday appliances that run on electricity. Its guidance adds that pupils should be taught to work safely with electricity. This lesson brings that forward for 6 and 7 year olds, who already use switches, sockets and battery toys. The words to use are mains electricity, socket, plug, cable, switch, appliance, battery and electric shock.","The science behind the rules: mains electricity in the UK is about 230 volts (about 120 volts in the US). An AA battery is 1.5 volts. Tap water and wet skin let electricity into the body far more easily than dry skin. That is why UK bathrooms use pull-cord switches and have no ordinary sockets near the bath. Metal is a good conductor, so a fork in a plugged-in toaster can carry electricity straight to a hand. Power lines carry thousands of volts, and the electricity can jump a gap. A kite string does not have to touch the line to be dangerous.","Common mistakes to watch for: thinking a battery toy and a wall socket are the same, or that wet hands are fine if you are quick. Pulling a plug out by its cable strains the wires inside until they break. Too many adaptors in one socket can overheat and start a fire. Children also need to hear that small batteries are safe to hold but must never go in the mouth. A swallowed button battery can burn the throat within hours.","In the lab your child sorts six things into plug-in or battery, tests how strong a battery and a wall socket are, dries wet hands and moves a hairdryer away from the sink, spots three dangers in a kitchen, switches off and pulls a plug out the safe way, and drags a kite away from power lines. Every step works with big buttons as well as taps and drags. Read the Challenge aloud together."]},"familyTip":"Walk round your home together and find three things that plug in and three that use batteries. Then show your child where the switch for each socket is, how to switch off before unplugging, and which grown-up to tell if a cable looks damaged.","keyIdea":"Some things plug into a socket in the wall. That is mains electricity, and it is strong enough to hurt you badly. Other things, like a torch or a remote, use small batteries, which are much weaker. Keep water away from plugs and switches, and dry wet hands first. Never put metal or fingers into a socket or a toaster. To unplug, switch off and pull the plug, not the cable. Tell a grown-up about a broken cable or too many plugs in one socket. Fly kites far away from power lines.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe or not safe? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe","Not safe"],"items":[["Dry hands on a switch",0],["Holding a torch",0],["Pulling out the plug",0],["A fork in a toaster",1],["Wet hands on a plug",1],["A kite by power lines",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagzap","caption":"Flagship lab: plug or battery, battery against socket, water, kitchen dangers, unplugging safely and kites near power lines.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_iGSfQg3g5c":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does sunlight do to the ground?","choices":["it warms it","it cools it","nothing: it only gives light"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of how a path feels after the sun shines on it all day.","explain":"Sunlight gives warmth as well as light, so the ground it shines on warms up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is it cooler under a tree on a sunny day?","choices":["the leaves block the sunlight","the tree makes cold air","trees are wet"],"answer":0,"hint":"What stops the sunlight from reaching the ground there?","explain":"The leaves block the sunlight and make shade. The tree does not make cold; it just stops the sun warming that spot."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Sand and water sit in the sun all morning. Which feels hotter?","choices":["the sand","the water","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the beach: the sand and the sea on a sunny day.","explain":"The sand warms up fast and feels hot. Water warms up slowly, so it feels cooler."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"When is a sunny slide hottest?","choices":["in the middle of the day","early in the morning","at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"When is the sun high in the sky?","explain":"In the middle of the day the sun is high and has shone on the slide for hours, so it is hottest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which roof keeps a dog's water bowl cool in the sun?","choices":["an umbrella","clear plastic","a net with big holes"],"answer":0,"hint":"The roof needs to stop the sunlight, not let it through.","explain":"An umbrella blocks the sunlight and makes shade. Clear plastic and a net let lots of sunlight through."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You want to test if colour matters in the sun. Where should the dark can and the white can go?","choices":["both in the sun","one in the sun and one in the shade","both in the shade"],"answer":0,"hint":"Keep everything the same except the colour.","explain":"Put both in the sun so only the colour is different. Then the dark can warms more, and you know colour did it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{a} children sit in the sun and {b} sit under a big umbrella. How many children are in the shade?","answer":"b","hint":"The umbrella blocks the sunlight. Who is under it?","explain":"The {b} children under the umbrella are in the shade. The {a} children in the sun will feel warmer.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A path has 10 stones. A tree shades {a} of them. How many stones are in the sun?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Take the shaded stones away from all 10.","explain":"10 − {a} = {=10-a}, so {=10-a} stones are in the sun and get warmer.","params":{"a":[3,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Two toy cars sit outside. One is in the sun and one is in the shade. Which one gets warmer?","choices":["the one in the sun","the one in the shade","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which car the sunlight shines on.","explain":"Sunlight shines on the car in the sun and warms it. The shade blocks the sunlight, so that car stays cooler."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sandpit has 10 squares. An umbrella shades {a} of them. How many squares are still in the sun?","answer":"10-a","hint":"Start with all 10 squares and take away the shaded ones.","explain":"10 − {a} = {=10-a}, so {=10-a} squares are still in the sun and will warm up more.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"There are {a} stones in the sun and {b} stones in the shade. How many stones will get hot?","answer":"a","hint":"Only the things the sunlight shines on get hot.","explain":"Only the {a} stones in the sun get hot. The {b} stones in the shade stay cooler, because the sunlight is blocked.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which T-shirt gets hotter in the sun?","choices":["the black one","the white one","both the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dark colours take in more sunlight than light colours.","explain":"The black T-shirt takes in more of the sunlight, so it gets hotter. The white one sends more of the light away."}],"explain":{"kid":["The sun gives us light. It gives us warmth too. When sunlight shines on the ground, the ground gets warm.","Shade is a place where the sunlight is blocked. A tree, a roof or an umbrella can make shade. A stone in the sun gets hot. A stone in the shade stays cool. Shade does not make things cold. It just stops the sun.","Some things warm up faster. A dark T-shirt gets hotter than a white one. Sand gets hot fast, but water warms up slowly. That is why the sand can burn your feet while the sea feels cool.","In the morning the sun is low, and the slide is cool. In the middle of the day the sun is high, and the slide can be hot. A big umbrella can keep a spot cool."],"adult":["This matches two US kindergarten standards. K-PS3-1 asks children to 'make observations to determine the effect of sunlight on Earth's surface'. K-PS3-2 asks them to 'use tools and materials to design and build a structure that will reduce the warming effect of sunlight on an area'. At this age children compare warmer and cooler. They do not need to read degrees.","The science: sunlight carries energy, and a surface that takes it in warms up. Shade is where something blocks the direct sunlight, so less energy lands there. Warm air still warms shaded things a little. Dark surfaces take in more light than pale ones. Water warms more slowly than sand, as it needs more energy to heat and it mixes the heat down through its depth.","Common mistakes: thinking shade or a tree 'makes cold' instead of blocking sunlight, thinking the sun only gives light, and expecting a clear roof to keep things cool. Children also compare two things that differ in more than one way. The ground is often hottest in the early afternoon, a little after the sun is highest, as it keeps soaking up sunlight.","In the lab your child puts one stone in the sun and one in the shade and waits until midday. Next they compare a dark and a white can and feel sand and water. Then they drag the sun across the sky to warm and cool a slide, and test roofs until one keeps a dog's water bowl cool."]},"familyTip":"On a sunny day, put two of the same toy outside, one in the sun and one in the shade. Feel them after lunch. Touch a dark car and a white car, or the sand and the sea, and talk about which is hotter and why. Then build a shade for a toy with a towel.","keyIdea":"Sunlight warms the ground, the water and the things it shines on. A thing in the sun warms up more than the same thing in the shade, because shade is just a place where something blocks the sunlight. Dark things and sand warm up faster than white things and water, the ground is warmest in the middle of the day, and a roof that blocks the sun keeps a place cooler.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it get warm in the sun, or does it make shade? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Gets warm in the sun","Blocks the sun to make shade"],"items":[["sand",0],["a dark car",0],["a metal slide",0],["a rock",0],["a tree",1],["an umbrella",1],["a sun hat",1],["a roof",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsun","caption":"Flagship lab: compare sun and shade, dark and light, sand and water, follow the sun across the sky and build a roof that keeps water cool.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_NzCNuABT3E":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What must a circuit have for current to flow?","choices":["a complete loop with no gaps","one wire from the cell to the bulb","a switch that is open"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace the path from one end of the cell back to the other end.","explain":"Current only flows round a complete circuit: a full loop from one end of the cell, through the parts, back to the other end."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which component changes electrical energy into sound?","choices":["a buzzer","a motor","a cell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a door bell or an alarm.","explain":"A buzzer changes electrical energy into sound. A motor gives movement, and a cell gives the push."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You add a second bulb in series with the first. What happens?","choices":["both bulbs are dimmer","both bulbs are brighter","nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two bulbs in one loop give more resistance.","explain":"In series the two bulbs share one path. More resistance means less current, so both are dimmer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In a house, one light can be off while the others are on. How are the lights wired?","choices":["in parallel","in series","with no switches"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of circuit lets one bulb go out while the rest stay on?","explain":"They are in parallel: each light has its own branch and switch, so one can be off while the others stay on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why are wires made of copper with a plastic coat?","choices":["copper conducts and plastic insulates","plastic conducts and copper insulates","both block the current"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a metal, and what do metals do to current?","explain":"Copper is a metal, so it is a good conductor and carries the current. Plastic is an insulator, so it keeps the current off your hands."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does the voltage of a cell tell you?","choices":["how big a push it gives","how long its wires are","how bright the room is"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cell pushes the current round the loop.","explain":"Voltage is the size of the push the cell gives. A bigger voltage pushes more current through the same bulb, so it glows brighter."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Fairy lights have {a} bulbs in parallel. {b} bulbs are broken. How many still shine?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"In parallel a broken bulb only stops its own branch.","explain":"Each bulb has its own branch, so only the {b} broken ones are dark. {a} − {b} = {=a-b} still shine.","params":{"a":[8,20],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A battery is made of {a} cells joined in a row. How many cells do {b} of these batteries have?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Each battery has {a} cells. Count them for every battery.","explain":"{b} batteries × {a} cells = {=a*b} cells in all.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which word means two or more cells joined together?","choices":["a battery","a bulb","a switch"],"answer":0,"hint":"One cell gives a push. Join cells and you get a bigger push.","explain":"A battery is two or more cells joined together. One single unit on its own is a cell."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A parallel circuit has {a} bulbs, each on its own branch. You unscrew {b} of them. How many bulbs stay lit?","answer":"a-b","where":"b<a","hint":"In parallel each bulb has its own complete branch. Only the unscrewed ones go out.","explain":"Each branch is its own loop, so only the {b} unscrewed bulbs go out. {a} − {b} = {=a-b} bulbs stay lit.","params":{"a":[3,6],"b":[1,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A series circuit has {a} bulbs in one loop. One of them is unscrewed. How many bulbs stay lit?","answer":"0","hint":"In series there is only one path. What does one gap do to that path?","explain":"Unscrewing one bulb makes a gap in the only path, so the current stops and all {a} bulbs go out. 0 stay lit.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A circuit has a cell, a bulb and an open switch. What does the bulb do?","choices":["it stays off","it lights","it flickers"],"answer":0,"hint":"An open switch makes a gap. Can the current get round?","explain":"An open switch makes a gap, so the circuit is not complete. No current flows and the bulb stays off."}],"explain":{"kid":["A circuit is a loop that electricity flows round. Each part of it is a component. A cell gives the push. Two or more cells joined make a battery. A bulb gives light, a buzzer makes a sound and a motor spins. Wires join them up.","The flow is called current. The push from the cell is the voltage. Current only flows round a complete circuit. That means a full loop with no gaps, from one end of the cell, through the parts, and back to the other end. Wires are made of metal because metals are conductors. Plastic is an insulator, so it blocks the flow.","A switch is a gap you can control. When it is closed, the circuit is complete and the bulb lights. When it is open, there is a gap, so the bulb goes off. Open does not let the electricity out.","In a series circuit all the parts are in one loop. Add a second bulb and both get dimmer, because two bulbs give more resistance and less current flows. Take one bulb out and they all go off. In a parallel circuit each bulb has its own branch. Each one is as bright as one bulb alone, and if one goes out, the rest stay on."],"adult":["This lesson matches the English national curriculum for Year 4 Electricity: children should 'construct a simple series electrical circuit, identifying and naming its basic parts, including cells, wires, bulbs, switches and buzzers', and 'recognise that a switch opens and closes a circuit'. It also adds the Year 6 idea that brightness depends on the number of cells and bulbs, and the words series and parallel.","The key terms are component, cell, battery, current, voltage, resistance, conductor, insulator, switch, complete circuit, series and parallel. In everyday speech a single AA cell is called a battery. In science a battery is two or more cells joined, and the cell is the single unit.","Common mistakes: thinking one wire from the cell is enough, thinking an open switch lets the current through, and thinking the bulb uses up the current. Another slip is to believe that a second bulb in series gets the same current as one bulb alone. In fact both bulbs are dimmer. In parallel each branch gets the full voltage of the cell, so each bulb is as bright as one alone, but the cell gives more current in total and runs down faster.","In the lab your learner finds six components by name, closes two gaps to make a complete circuit and works a switch. Then they add a second bulb in series and in parallel and unscrew one, to see which bulbs stay on. Please be careful at home: mains electricity is never used for experiments, only small cells."]},"familyTip":"Look at a set of fairy lights or a torch together. Ask: is there a switch? Where does it make a gap? If one fairy light goes out and the rest stay on, are the bulbs in series or in parallel? Count the cells in a torch or a remote and talk about why more cells give a bigger push.","keyIdea":"Each part of a circuit is a component. A cell gives the push, called voltage; a battery is two or more cells joined. Current is the flow of electricity, and it only flows round a complete circuit, a loop of conductors with no gaps. A switch opens and closes a gap. In a series circuit everything sits in one loop, so extra bulbs add resistance, every bulb is dimmer and one gap stops them all. In a parallel circuit each bulb has its own branch, so each is as bright as one bulb alone and the others stay on if one is removed.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Series or parallel? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Series: one loop","Parallel: a branch each"],"items":[["one gap stops every bulb",0],["adding a bulb dims them all",0],["only one path for the current",0],["each bulb has its own branch",1],["one bulb out, the rest stay on",1],["each bulb gets the full voltage",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagseries","caption":"Flagship lab: find six components, close the gaps to complete a circuit, work a switch, then add a second bulb in series and in parallel and unscrew one.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
